  "cases": [
    {
      "case": "happy",
      "summary": "Real ovis-terminal serve with the QA output ring seam (--qa-output-ring-bytes 65536 on a qa-fixture root): hello advertises the session-wide and per-object budgets (64 MiB output ring, 256 KiB/1 MiB credit, 16 MiB input queue, 4 GiB retained, 64 MiB input/backlog, 128 MiB VT). /bin/sh's prompt is matched and the raw bytes at its matched_at read back as \"$ \" (offsets are relative to that run's prompt: a program may emit invisible bytes first, e.g. Darwin bash's 8-byte ESC[?1034h); an interact writer and an observe-only viewer attach and get the identical first paint. The writer's input.send is admitted with the output cursor captured at the prompt's end (before delivery), the evaluated marker mark-42 is matched exactly 20 bytes (the echoed command line) later and the raw bytes at matched_at read back as mark-42, and the barrier on its queued sequence is reached with 0 pending bytes. An operator is held behind the writer with a lease_released retry boundary naming the writer's lease, takes over by the observed epoch, and the writer's stream is told checkpoint, input_ready, input_revoked, close taken_over by the operator. 300 000 bytes then overflow the 64 KiB ring while the observer never credits: the operator's marker still matches, the observer's stream never passes its 262 144-byte credit, and its repaint answers (at the repaint's own stream offset, which the gap and checkpoint records repeat) a gap record (skipped raw range ending at terminal.screen's output offset) followed by a checkpoint equal to terminal.screen's offset and revision. terminal.output from 0 resumes at the retained floor with gap true (floor = head - 65 536), a read from the floor has no gap and ends at the head, and server.metrics accounts exactly one resynced byte loss to the observer and none to the operator. A second program (t2) writes a UTF-8 character and a CSI sequence split across separate writes and sends a DSR query (ESC[6n) the server answers: it sees the reply exactly once (REPLY-ONCE), a late observer's first paint and its repaint are exact (inexact: []), and the next byte the program reads is the operator's 'x', not a second reply. The raw bytes from offset 0 and terminal.screen are kept for an independent VT oracle (the Rust test feeds the raw bytes in one piece to ovis-terminal-vt and compares lines and cursor). The exact 16-PTY latency profile is capacity/sixteen.",
